const TIER={1:['p1','Warm-up'],2:['p2','FEA core'],3:['p5','Implicit'],4:['p4','Explicit'],5:['p3','Exterior'],6:['p6','Materials'],7:['p7','HyperWorks & process'],8:['pT','Test & reporting'],9:['p9','Hard / personal']};

const QS=[
/* ---------- 1 · WARM-UP ---------- */
{t:1,w:'Gustavo',q:"Tell us about yourself.",a:`<p>This is the CAE version. Analysis comes first, design second. It runs <strong>about 90 seconds</strong>.</p>
<div class="say">I'm a mechanical engineer with an MSc from Porto, a minor in aeronautical and vehicle structures, and a semester at Politecnico di Milano on FEA, CFD and structural reliability. The common thread in my work is building a model and then checking it against reality. My MSc thesis was at Volvo Cars: a multi-scale, strain-rate-dependent FE model of lithium-ion cells under crash, calibrated on layer-level tests I ran myself. It improved accuracy by a third and cut the calibration parameters by about two thirds. Through the degree I also did explicit drop-test work on a helicopter fuselage and implicit wing-box and fatigue work. In industry I've been on both sides of the model. At Apple I did failure analysis on iPhones in the field, root-causing a failure that affected over a million users. At Tesla I designed test equipment and ran a thermal study, unrequested, that reversed a design decision before freeze. I joined Helsing in October on the design side. What I've learned from all of it is that the analysis is the part I'm best at and want to do full time, and exterior CAE on a carbon hypercar is the most interesting version of that I can think of.</div>
<p><strong>Then stop.</strong> If they want the Helsing part expanded, they will ask. Use the 35-second script.</p>`},
{t:1,w:'Gustavo',q:"Why Bugatti Rimac, and why exterior CAE?",a:`<div class="say">Two reasons. First, the product. A car designed to be stable above 400 km/h, built mostly in carbon, with an active wing that doubles as an airbrake, and only 250 of them. At that volume you can't afford many prototypes or tooling changes, so simulation carries more of the sign-off than at a volume OEM, and the CAE engineer's judgement matters more. Second, exterior specifically. It's where stiffness, stability, impact, aero loads and durability all meet on parts customers can see and touch. It covers implicit and explicit, so it uses everything I've learned rather than one narrow slice.</div>
<div class="dont">&ldquo;Dream car since childhood&rdquo; &middot; Mate Rimac fandom &middot; &ldquo;cutting-edge&rdquo; &middot; anything that applies equally to Ferrari</div>`},
{t:1,w:'Gustavo',q:"What do you know about what our team does?",a:`<p>Keep it short and show the inference. Then turn it into a question.</p>
<div class="say">From the posting and the car: CAE for the exterior, so closures like the dihedral doors and the lids, the aero devices like the active wing and the diffuser, panels and attachments, implicit for stiffness, sag, dent and durability, and explicit for impact and slam. And since CAE and Materials sit together, I'd guess you also own the material cards, which matters a lot with CFRP. Is that roughly the split, or does the team cover more?</div>`},
{t:1,w:'Gustavo',q:"Where do you see yourself in five years?",a:`<div class="say">As a CAE engineer the design team comes to before they commit geometry, not after. Deep in exterior, trusted to own a whole system's load cases, and good at correlation, so the models are believed. I'm not chasing a management title. If leading a small team comes with the technical depth I'd take it, but not instead of it.</div>`},
{t:1,w:'Gustavo',q:"What are you working on at the moment?",a:`<p>Factual, one sentence, then pivot.</p>
<div class="say">I joined Helsing in Munich in October as a mechanical engineer on the design side, onboarding and getting into their hardware. Most of what's relevant to this role is from before: the Volvo work and the analysis I've done along the way.</div>
<p>If they push, go to <em>! The Helsing question</em>.</p>`},
{t:1,w:'Any',q:"Walk us through your thesis at a high level.",a:`<p>Use <em>&sect;5 Story 1</em>. Open with the scope sentence, then offer depth: &ldquo;Happy to go into the homogenisation or the calibration, whichever is more useful.&rdquo; That hands Ivan the choice and shows you can go deep on either.</p>`},

/* ---------- 2 · FEA CORE ---------- */
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"Explain the difference between implicit and explicit solvers.",a:`<p>Full answer and three levels of follow-up in <strong>&sect;8</strong>. Core points: equation of motion; Newton&ndash;Raphson with tangent stiffness vs central difference with lumped mass; unconditional vs conditional stability; &Delta;t &le; L<sub>e</sub>/c; which load cases each suits.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"What are the sources of nonlinearity in FEA? Give an exterior example of each.",a:`<ul><li><strong>Material:</strong> plasticity in a steel hinge under overslam; thermoplastic fascia; hyperelastic seal.</li>
<li><strong>Geometric:</strong> large displacement and stress stiffening; oil-canning snap-through of a shallow panel; buckling of a thin flange.</li>
<li><strong>Boundary / contact:</strong> door to seal; latch to striker; indenter to panel; friction.</li></ul>
<p>Follow-up: &ldquo;How do you know if you need geometric nonlinearity?&rdquo; If displacements change the load path or stiffness (deflection comparable to thickness for plates, rotations above a few degrees), or if a linear result shows deflection much larger than the thickness of a thin panel. Run both and compare.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"Shear locking: what is it and how do you avoid it?",a:`<div class="say">Fully integrated linear elements can't represent pure bending. To bend they have to develop parasitic shear strain, which makes them far too stiff, so a beam in bending predicts a fraction of the real deflection. Fixes: reduced integration with hourglass control, incompatible-mode elements, quadratic elements, or several elements through the thickness for solids. For thin structures, use shell elements, which are formulated for bending.</div>
<p>Related: <strong>volumetric locking</strong> in nearly incompressible behaviour (plasticity, rubber). Use hybrid or mixed formulations, or reduced or selective integration.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"What is hourglassing, and how do you control and check it?",a:`<p>See <em>&sect;8</em>. Key points: zero-energy modes in reduced-integration elements; controlled by hourglass stiffness or viscosity; check hourglass energy against internal energy (typical target &lt; 5&ndash;10%), both globally and per part. Causes include point loads, coarse mesh in bending, and concentrated contact. Visual sign: zig-zag mesh deformation.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"How do you know your mesh is fine enough?",a:`<div class="say">A convergence study on the quantity I'm reporting, not on the whole model. Refine in the region of interest and plot the result against element size. When the change between refinements falls below a few percent, the mesh is fine enough for that output. Displacements converge fast, stresses slowly, and stress at a singularity never does. If stress keeps rising with every refinement, it's a singularity, not a converging peak. I did this on the thesis and checked energy balance alongside it.</div>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"You see a very high stress at a corner. Is it real?",a:`<div class="say">First I check whether it's a singularity: a sharp re-entrant corner, a point load, a point constraint, or the edge of a rigid element. If refining the mesh makes it keep rising, it's numerical. Then I either model the real fillet radius, read the stress a couple of elements away, or use a method meant for it, like hot-spot stress for welds. If it converges with refinement and the geometry is real, it's a real peak and it goes into the assessment, for static strength and especially fatigue.</div>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"RBE2 vs RBE3: when do you use each?",a:`<p>RBE2 is rigid and adds infinite stiffness between nodes. Use it for genuinely rigid connections (bolt through a thick boss, a stiff casting). RBE3 interpolates: it distributes force and mass to the nodes by weighting, without adding stiffness. Use it to apply loads or lumped masses. <strong>Trap:</strong> applying a load to a thin CFRP panel through an RBE2 makes the panel artificially stiff over the spider's footprint and underpredicts deflection.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"Shells or solids: how do you decide, and what are the pitfalls of shells?",a:`<p>Shells when thickness is small compared with in-plane dimensions (around 1:10 or less): sheet metal, CFRP skins, plastic fascias. Solids for castings, thick bosses, inserts, adhesive bead detail. Shell pitfalls: mid-surface offsets (the mesh is at the mid-plane but contact is at the outer surface, so set thickness offsets for contact); thickness changes; T-junction double-counting; through-thickness stress is not available; enough integration points through the thickness for plasticity (5 is common).</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"How would you model a bolted joint?",a:`<div class="say">It depends on the question. For global stiffness, a beam or CBUSH element between the parts, connected with RBE2 spiders sized to the washer or head footprint. For joint loads or separation, add preload, with contact between the clamped faces so they can open. For local stress in the boss, solid bolt and threads simplified, contact under the head, and a pretension section. Into CFRP I'd also check bearing stress and whether there's a metallic insert. Preload relaxation in composite is a real issue.</div>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"How would you model an adhesive bond between CFRP panels?",a:`<p>Options by fidelity: (1) <strong>tied contact</strong>, which gives stiffness but no bond failure; (2) <strong>cohesive elements</strong> with a traction&ndash;separation law (initiation stress, fracture energies G<sub>Ic</sub>/G<sub>IIc</sub>, mixed-mode law), for debond prediction; (3) solid adhesive layer for detailed stress. For exterior slam and impact, debond is a real failure mode, so cohesive is justified where peel loads are high. Check: cohesive elements need small elements in the process zone, and explicit time-step sensitivity if the thickness is small.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"Linear buckling analysis: what does it give you and when is it misleading?",a:`<p>An eigenvalue problem: (K + &lambda;K<sub>G</sub>)&phi; = 0, giving a load multiplier and a mode shape. It is misleading for imperfection-sensitive structures (curved shells, cylinders), where real collapse can be far below the linear prediction, and where plasticity happens before buckling. Better: nonlinear analysis with an imperfection seeded from the first mode or modes (amplitude around a fraction of thickness), plus arc-length to pass the limit point.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"What checks do you run before trusting any result?",a:`<ol><li>Units consistent (mm, t, s, N, MPa is the classic set). Mass versus CAD.</li><li>Free-free modal: six rigid-body modes near 0 Hz, a seventh means something is disconnected.</li><li>Reaction forces equal applied loads.</li><li>Deformed shape plausible at true scale and exaggerated.</li><li>Implicit: convergence history. Explicit: energy balance, hourglass, added mass, time step.</li><li>Compare with the hand calculation done beforehand.</li><li>Mesh sensitivity at the location being reported.</li></ol>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"What is inertia relief and when would you use it?",a:`<p>For a free body under applied loads (a vehicle or subsystem with no real constraints), inertia relief balances the applied loads with D'Alembert inertial forces from the mass distribution. The model is in equilibrium without arbitrary constraints, so there are no artificial reactions or stress concentrations at fake supports. Used for road-load durability on bodies and subsystems, and aero load cases on free structures.</p>`},
{t:2,w:'Ivan',q:"What element would you use for a thin CFRP panel, and how do you define the layup?",a:`<p>A layered (composite) shell: CQUAD4 with PCOMP (zone-based) or PCOMPP plus STACK and PLY (ply-based) in OptiStruct. MAT8 orthotropic ply properties. Material orientation set per element or by projection, since fibre angle matters. Ply-based follows real plies across zones and makes drop-off and optimisation sensible. Output per-ply stresses and strains and failure indices. Check the laminate's ABD behaviour (unbalanced or unsymmetric layups couple bending and extension).</p>`},

/* ---------- 3 · IMPLICIT ---------- */
{t:3,w:'Ivan',q:"Explain Newton–Raphson. What do you do when it doesn't converge?",a:`<div class="say">At each increment you linearise about the current state, solve K<sub>T</sub>&Delta;u = R for a correction, update, recompute the residual and iterate until the residual and the displacement correction fall below tolerance. Convergence is quadratic near the solution if the tangent is consistent. When it fails I look at where and why. Contact chattering: smaller increments, contact stabilisation, cleaner initial gaps. Material softening or buckling: the tangent goes singular at a limit point, so switch to displacement control or arc-length. Large increments: let the automatic stepping cut back. And if the event is really dynamic with lots of contact, the honest answer may be that it belongs in explicit.</div>`},
{t:3,w:'Ivan',q:"What is the arc-length (Riks) method and when do you need it?",a:`<p>It controls the increment by a combined measure of load and displacement (the arc length) instead of load alone. That lets the solver follow the equilibrium path through limit points where the load drops (snap-through), and through snap-back where displacement also reverses. Needed for post-buckling, collapse and <strong>oil-canning</strong> of shallow panels. Displacement control alone handles snap-through but not snap-back. See drill 12.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Gustavo',q:"How would you set up a door sag analysis?",a:`<p>Use the 10-step checklist in &sect;7.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Target:</strong> elastic and permanent set at the latch or striker under specified load (company spec).</li><li><strong>Model:</strong> door assembly (inner, outer, reinforcements, hinge reinforcements), hinges with pin or revolute joints, check strap or stop in its open position. Body side either fixed at the hinge mounting, or with a cut-out of the body-in-white if hinge-pillar stiffness matters, and it usually does.</li><li><strong>Load:</strong> vertical force at the outer end (distributed with RBE3) plus gravity, at the specified opening angle.</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> nonlinear static (plasticity at the hinge reinforcement), load then unload to read permanent set.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> deflection at the latch, permanent set, plastic strain at the hinges.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Dihedral-door twist:</strong> the hinge axis is inclined, so gravity and the actuator moment vary through the arc. Check several opening angles and the actuator and hinge reactions. Ask how they define it.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Gustavo',q:"How would you assess the stiffness of a bonnet or frunk lid?",a:`<p>Linear static. Constrain at the hinges and latch (or latch plus bump stops, matching the test). Torsion: load at one free corner, others constrained. Bending: load mid-span. Report stiffness as N/mm at the load point. Also check the inner panel's contribution and adhesive or hem modelling. For the Tourbillon's <strong>through-flow bonnet</strong>, openings reduce torsional stiffness, so look at stress and deformation around them. Combine with modal (first torsion and bending) for buffeting at speed.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Ivan',q:"How would you model dent resistance and oil-canning on an outer panel?",a:`<p>Rigid indenter (hemispherical or flat pad, per spec) pushed into the panel at grid points under displacement control, with contact. Nonlinear geometric (and material, for permanent dent in metals). Output the load-deflection curve. Stiffness is the initial slope; <strong>oil-canning</strong> shows as a limit point or flat region (snap-through), and permanent set after unload gives the dent. Load-control Newton fails at the limit point, so use displacement control or arc-length. For CFRP the permanent dent is less of an issue than local stiffness, snap-through and core or matrix damage if it is a sandwich.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Ivan',q:"Modal analysis: how is it solved and what do you check?",a:`<p>Generalised eigenproblem (K &minus; &omega;&sup2;M)&phi; = 0, solved with Lanczos (EIGRL in OptiStruct) for the lowest modes. Checks: mass matches; free-free run gives six rigid-body modes; mode shapes physically sensible; local panel modes not hidden among global ones. Use: keep the first modes of mirrors, the wing and panels above excitation bands (engine orders, road, aero buffeting). Correlation: frequency error within a few percent and MAC &gt; about 0.9.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Gustavo',q:"How would you approach a durability analysis of an exterior bracket?",a:`<ol><li>Load source: road-load data (measured or multibody), slam cycles, or aero.</li><li>Unit-load or modal-transient stress solutions (linear, OptiStruct), combined with the load time histories by superposition.</li><li>Rainflow counting, then S-N (high cycle) or &epsilon;-N (local plasticity), with mean-stress correction (Goodman for conservative, Gerber, or Smith&ndash;Watson&ndash;Topper in &epsilon;-N).</li><li>Miner's rule damage; target damage &lt; 1 with a safety factor.</li><li>Surface finish, size and notch factors; welds use their own S-N classes.</li></ol>
<p>Anchor: your A400M work (mission profile, S-N, NASGRO, 489 h and 770 h intervals). That is the same structure for aircraft.</p>`},
{t:3,w:'Vijay',q:"How would you apply CFD pressure loads to the rear wing in FE?",a:`<p>Map the CFD surface pressure onto the FE surface mesh (HyperMesh and other pre-processors have mapping tools), then check the integrated total force and moment against the CFD result. Mapping loses fidelity when meshes differ a lot. Run several flight states: max downforce at the relevant speed, airbrake deployed at high speed, and yaw or crosswind cases. Check stiffness (aero shape change changes the load, so iterate or couple if it is significant) and actuator and hinge reactions. Add modal, since buffeting and flutter are possible on a slender wing.</p>`},

/* ---------- 4 · EXPLICIT ---------- */
{t:4,w:'Ivan',q:"Derive or explain the explicit stable time step.",a:`<p>Central difference is stable for &Delta;t &le; 2/&omega;<sub>max</sub>. For a single element &omega;<sub>max</sub> &asymp; 2c/L<sub>e</sub>, so &Delta;t &le; L<sub>e</sub>/c, with c = &radic;(E/&rho;) for a bar (plate wave speed &radic;(E/&rho;(1&minus;&nu;&sup2;)) for shells). Physically, a wave must not cross more than one element per step. See drill 1 for numbers.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Vijay',q:"What is mass scaling? When is it acceptable?",a:`<p>See &sect;8 and drill 2. Acceptable when the added mass is a small fraction of the total (often &lt; 2&ndash;5%) and it is in small elements away from where inertia drives the result. Never in the impact zone of a dynamic event. Always report it. Radioss offers nodal time-step control (/DT/NODA) as the equivalent.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Ivan',q:"Which energies do you check in an explicit run and what do you look for?",a:`<ul><li><strong>Total energy:</strong> constant (energy ratio near 1). Growth means instability or a bad contact.</li><li><strong>Internal vs kinetic:</strong> follow the physics. For quasi-static explicit, kinetic &lt; 5&ndash;10% of internal.</li><li><strong>Hourglass:</strong> &lt; 5&ndash;10% of internal, globally and per part.</li><li><strong>Contact or sliding energy:</strong> small and positive. Large negative values mean penetration problems.</li><li><strong>Added mass:</strong> reported, small.</li></ul><p>See drill 11.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Ivan',q:"Penalty contact vs Lagrange multiplier: what is the difference?",a:`<p><strong>Penalty:</strong> a spring is inserted when penetration is detected. Force is proportional to penetration, small penetration is allowed, and the stiffness is scaled from the element stiffness. Cheap and the default in explicit. Too stiff reduces the stable time step or causes noise; too soft gives visible penetration. <strong>Lagrange:</strong> the constraint is enforced exactly with extra unknowns. Used in implicit, more expensive. Also: node-to-surface vs surface-to-surface (more robust for shell edges), and self-contact for folding.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Vijay',q:"How would you set up a low-speed bumper/fascia impact?",a:`<ol><li>Impactor or pendulum per the regulation or standard (mass, height, velocity), modelled rigid with the correct mass.</li><li>Fascia, absorber (foam or plastic), beam and mounting with real connections.</li><li>Materials: strain-rate-dependent thermoplastic cards (tabulated, e.g. Radioss LAW36), foam (crushable), metals (Johnson&ndash;Cook or tabulated).</li><li>General contact plus self-contact. Initial penetrations cleaned.</li><li>Output: intrusion, permanent damage to lamps, beam and mounts, force-time, energy balance.</li></ol><p>Hand check: impact energy &frac12;mv&sup2; (drill 10).</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Ivan',q:"How do you model strain-rate dependence in a material?",a:`<p>Either a law with a rate term, such as Johnson&ndash;Cook &sigma; = (A + B&epsilon;<sup>n</sup>)(1 + C ln &epsilon;&#775;*)(1 &minus; T*<sup>m</sup>) or Cowper&ndash;Symonds &sigma;<sub>d</sub>/&sigma;<sub>s</sub> = 1 + (&epsilon;&#775;/D)<sup>1/q</sup>, or tabulated stress-strain curves at several strain rates, interpolated. Rate is usually filtered to avoid noise. Anchor: your thesis did this for the cell layers. Be ready to say which form you used and over what strain-rate range you characterised.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Gustavo',q:"Door slam: how would you approach it?",a:`<p>Explicit transient of the door closing at a specified velocity, with seal compression modelled (force-deflection curve or a hyperelastic seal), latch engagement, and contact. Extract stress and strain histories at critical locations (hinge reinforcements, inner panel corners, mirror mount, adhesive joints). Then fatigue over the specified number of cycles. For powered or soft-close doors, the impact is lower but actuator end-stop loads matter. Correlate peak accelerations and strains against a slam rig.</p>`},
{t:4,w:'Ivan',q:"How would you model crash of a CFRP structure, like the rear diffuser crash element?",a:`<p>Honest framing: you haven't done production CFRP crash, so show you know the issues. Progressive crushing is dominated by fragmentation, delamination and frond formation, which shell models capture only approximately. Options: layered shells with a progressive-damage material model (e.g. Radioss composite laws, Ladev&egrave;ze-type or Matzenmiller-type damage), stacked shells with cohesive interfaces for delamination, and a crush trigger. These models need calibration on coupon and component crush tests (specific energy absorption), and they are mesh-sensitive, so regularise. <strong>Say:</strong> &ldquo;This is where I'd lean on the team's correlated method rather than invent one.&rdquo;</p>`},

/* ---------- 5 · EXTERIOR ---------- */
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"Which load cases would you consider for an active rear wing?",a:`<ul><li><strong>Aero downforce</strong> at max speed with the wing deployed: stiffness (shape retention), strength, actuator load.</li><li><strong>Airbrake</strong>: high angle at high speed, sudden deployment. Dynamic amplification up to about 2 for a step load on a lightly damped structure (drill 3).</li><li><strong>Crosswind or yaw:</strong> asymmetric load, torsion of the mounts.</li><li><strong>Modal:</strong> first bending and torsion versus buffeting and engine excitation. Flutter is a coupled aero-structural issue to flag.</li><li><strong>Fatigue:</strong> deployment cycles plus road vibration.</li><li><strong>Abuse:</strong> someone leaning on or pushing the wing when parked, car-wash loads.</li><li><strong>Thermal:</strong> exhaust or engine heat near the mounts. CTE mismatch between CFRP blade and metal mechanism.</li></ul>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"What changes for a dihedral door compared with a conventional door?",a:`<ul><li>The hinge axis is inclined, so gravity's moment about the hinge varies through the opening arc. Sag is not one case but a function of angle.</li><li>The actuator and gas-strut loads must hold the door at every angle. The worst case may be partially open, in wind.</li><li>Wind gust on a fully open door has a long lever arm: hinge and A-pillar or roof loads.</li><li>Abuse: someone pulling the door down or forcing it past its stops. End-stop impact loads from the powered mechanism.</li><li>Seal and closing: the door closes downwards with gravity, which changes slam and latch engagement.</li><li>Crash interaction: hinge location affects how the door behaves in side impact and roll-over (egress requirements).</li></ul>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"What is oil-canning, and why does it matter to a customer?",a:`<p>Snap-through instability of a shallow, lightly curved panel. You press it, it suddenly pops inwards, and it may pop back. It is perceived as cheapness or fragility, so on a &euro;3.8M car it is unacceptable even if it is structurally harmless. Drivers: low curvature, thin skin, large unsupported span. Fixes: more curvature (styling permitting), local reinforcement or bonding to the inner, ribs or beads (OptiStruct topography optimisation suggests bead patterns), or a thicker or sandwich laminate.</p>`},
{t:5,w:'Any',q:"What is the dynamic pressure at 400 km/h and why does it matter?",a:`<p>q = &frac12;&rho;v&sup2; = 0.5 &times; 1.225 &times; (111.1)&sup2; &asymp; <strong>7.6 kPa</strong>. That is roughly 760 kg-force per square metre of pressure coefficient. Every exterior part (flaps, mirror housings, fender edges, trim) must keep its shape and stay attached under loads that scale with v&sup2;. At twice the speed, four times the load. Suction on upper surfaces can pull panels off their attachments, so check fastener and adhesive pull-off, not just bending.</p>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"Mirror vibration: what would you check?",a:`<p>First modes of the mirror assembly (arm plus head) above the road and engine excitation bands, typically aiming for a margin to avoid image blur. Frequency response to base excitation at the mount. Stiffness under aero load at top speed. Drill 5 gives the order of magnitude for an aluminium arm with a 1 kg head. Ask whether the Tourbillon uses glass mirrors or cameras. Do not assume.</p>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"How would you analyse gap and flush under temperature?",a:`<p>Thermal load case (hot soak, cold soak, paint-bake if relevant) on the assembly with the real attachments. The key driver is <strong>CTE mismatch</strong>: CFRP close to zero in-plane, aluminium about 23 &micro;m/m&middot;K, steel about 12. Over a 1 m panel and an 80 K swing, aluminium grows by about 1.8 mm against about 0.1&ndash;0.2 mm for CFRP. Results: gap and flush change at boundaries, stresses at constrained fixings, adhesive shear. Fixes: slotted or floating mounts, compliant attachments, a matched-CTE bracket material. Drill 9.</p>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"Pedestrian protection: does it apply to a car like this? What would you model?",a:`<p>Say you'd confirm the homologation route: requirements and exemptions depend on how a low-volume vehicle is type-approved and on the market. Technically: headform impactors (adult and child) on the bonnet and windscreen base giving HIC, and a leg impactor on the front bumper. Explicit, with impactor models certified by the suppliers. The challenge on a low, stiff, carbon front end is space for deformation over hard points (radiators, frame). The through-flow bonnet layout is relevant.</p>`},
{t:5,w:'Gustavo',q:"A designer wants to cut the outer skin thickness to save weight. How do you respond?",a:`<div class="say">I'd ask which requirement governs that panel, because it's probably not strength. For an outer skin it's usually stiffness, oil-canning, dent or palm load, or modal. Bending stiffness scales with thickness cubed, so a 20% reduction loses about half the bending stiffness. Before saying no, I'd run the governing cases at the new thickness and look at alternatives that keep the mass saving: local reinforcement, bonding to the inner at a new point, ply changes, or bead patterns. I'd come back with what passes and what each option costs in mass.</div>`},
{t:5,w:'Any',q:"Why is CAE more important at 250 cars a year than at 250,000?",a:`<div class="say">Because the cost of physical iterations is spread over so few cars. Composite tooling is expensive and slow to change, prototype cars are extremely expensive, and there are few of them, so you can't test everything physically or iterate on hardware. Simulation has to do more of the validation and the few physical tests have to be well chosen and well correlated. The flip side is that the CAE has to be trusted, which raises the bar on correlation and on knowing exactly how far you can believe a model.</div>`},

/* ---------- 6 · MATERIALS ---------- */
{t:6,w:'Gustavo',q:"Which failure criteria do you know for composites and how do they differ?",a:`<ul><li><strong>Max stress or max strain:</strong> each ply direction is checked independently. Simple, with no interaction.</li><li><strong>Tsai&ndash;Hill / Tsai&ndash;Wu:</strong> a single quadratic interaction polynomial. One index, but it doesn't say <em>which</em> mode failed. Tsai&ndash;Wu needs an interaction coefficient that is hard to measure.</li><li><strong>Hashin:</strong> separates fibre and matrix failure, in tension and compression.</li><li><strong>Puck:</strong> physically based, with fracture-plane angle for inter-fibre failure. Good at matrix compression.</li></ul><p>For exterior parts, first-ply failure is often conservative and stiffness governs. Delamination needs a separate approach (interlaminar stress or cohesive zones).</p>`},
{t:6,w:'Gustavo',q:"Where do material cards come from, and what makes a good one?",a:`<p>Coupon testing (tension, compression, shear, at rates for explicit; ply-level for CFRP), supplier data, or literature. Then calibration of the chosen model to those tests, and validation on a component test that was not used in fitting. A good card is <em>fit for the load case</em>: the right rate range, temperature, failure definition, and knowledge of the scatter. Link to the thesis: you ran the layer tests yourself and calibrated from them. That is exactly this process.</p>`},
{t:6,w:'Ivan',q:"Thermoplastic fascia material: what is special about modelling it?",a:`<p>Strong strain-rate dependence, different behaviour in tension and compression, pressure-dependent yield, temperature dependence (cold impacts are brittle), and failure that is hard to predict. Typically handled with tabulated rate-dependent curves (e.g. Radioss LAW36, or more advanced polymer laws), calibrated at several rates and temperatures. Mesh dependence of the failure strain should be regularised by element size.</p>`},
{t:6,w:'Gustavo',q:"What are the main differences between designing in CFRP and in aluminium, from a CAE view?",a:`<ul><li>Anisotropy, and the layup is a design variable (orientation, stacking, drop-offs).</li><li>Mostly linear-elastic to brittle failure, with no plastic reserve, so margins mean something different.</li><li>Joints: bonding and inserts instead of welds. Bearing and pull-out at fasteners.</li><li>Larger material scatter, and environmental knock-downs (hot/wet).</li><li>Damage tolerance: barely visible impact damage, delamination.</li><li>CTE close to zero creates mismatch with metal parts.</li><li>Optimisation: free-size &rarr; size &rarr; ply shuffle (the OptiStruct three-phase process).</li></ul>`},
{t:6,w:'Ivan',q:"Explain homogenisation, as you did in your thesis.",a:`<div class="say">You replace a heterogeneous microstructure, here the layered stack of anode, cathode, separator and current collectors, with an equivalent homogeneous material whose effective properties reproduce the response of a representative volume element. The RVE has to be large enough to be statistically representative, but small compared with the structure. You load it in the relevant modes, extract effective stiffness and the non-linear response, and use that as the material in the full model. That's why the number of calibration parameters dropped: they came from the constituents rather than being fitted at cell level.</div>
<p class="catch">Be ready for: &ldquo;What boundary conditions did you use on the RVE?&rdquo; (periodic, kinematic or traction). Know your answer.</p>`},

/* ---------- 7 · HYPERWORKS & PROCESS ---------- */
{t:7,w:'Vijay',q:"Have you used HyperWorks?",a:`<p>See <em>&sect;6 Gap 1</em> for the script. Short version: no production use; Abaqus Standard and Explicit plus 3DEXPERIENCE; you know the map (HyperMesh, OptiStruct, Radioss, HyperView, HyperStudy); a proven fast learner (3DEXPERIENCE in weeks). Do not overclaim.</p>`},
{t:7,w:'Vijay',q:"What mesh-quality criteria do you check?",a:`<p>Aspect ratio, warpage, skew, Jacobian, min and max angles for quads and trias, tria percentage, minimum element size or explicit time step, free edges, duplicates, and normal consistency for shells (important for contact and pressure loads). Values: see the table in &sect;9, and say you'd follow their guideline.</p>`},
{t:7,w:'Vijay',q:"How would you automate a repetitive load-case setup?",a:`<p>Template the deck: the same subcases, outputs and checks for each variant. Script the pre-processing (HyperMesh Tcl or Python API) to import the new geometry, assign properties from a naming convention, create connectors, apply loads at standard locations, and run the model checks. Script post-processing (HyperView) to extract standard results into a report template. Anchor: at Apple you scripted the processing of 10,000+ images. Same mindset: automate what is repeated, so the engineering time goes to judgement.</p>`},
{t:7,w:'Vijay',q:"How do you make sure a model you hand over can be reproduced by a colleague?",a:`<p>Naming conventions for parts, properties and materials. Units stated. Material card source and version recorded. Load-case definition documented against the spec. Model checks listed in the report. Files under version control or PDM. Assumptions written explicitly (&ldquo;measured vs assumed&rdquo;). The test: could someone rerun it from the report alone?</p>`},
{t:7,w:'Vijay',q:"What is topology / topography / free-size optimisation, and when would you use them on exterior parts?",a:`<ul><li><strong>Topology:</strong> material distribution in a design space. Concept stage for brackets and castings (3D printed or cast parts, which this car uses).</li><li><strong>Topography:</strong> bead or swage patterns on thin panels. Raises stiffness and modal frequency at no mass cost, useful against oil-canning.</li><li><strong>Free-size:</strong> continuous thickness, or per-ply-orientation thickness for composites. Phase 1 of the composite process, then size (discrete plies), then ply-stacking shuffle.</li><li><strong>Size and shape:</strong> fine-tuning thickness and geometry.</li></ul>`},
{t:7,w:'Gustavo',q:"How do you work with designers when the CAE result is bad news?",a:`<div class="say">Early and with an alternative. I'd rather show a designer a rough result before they've committed geometry than a precise one after. When it fails, I come with three things: what fails and by how much, what's driving it, and one or two options I've already checked. Having been the designer myself, I know a contour plot without a direction is useless to them. What they need is &ldquo;it fails here by 20%, and adding this rib or moving this fixing fixes it&rdquo;.</div>`},

/* ---------- 8 · TEST & REPORTING ---------- */
{t:8,w:'Gustavo',q:"How do you correlate a model to a physical test?",a:`<p>See <em>&sect;10</em>. Plan with the model, reproduce the actual test set-up, compare like with like, diagnose BCs first, change one thing at a time, validate on a test not used for calibration, then update the guideline.</p>`},
{t:8,w:'Gustavo',q:"Your simulation and test disagree by 30%. What do you do?",a:`<div class="say">I don't touch the material card first. I start with the test set-up versus the model: fixture stiffness, how it was really constrained, where the load really went in, measured thickness and ply count versus nominal, and whether the sensor sits where I'm reading. Most large discrepancies are boundary conditions or geometry. Then connections and contact, then the material card, then the mesh. One change at a time, and I note which test data I've used to tune, so I can still validate on something independent. And I'd also question the test. Thirty percent sometimes means a broken gauge or a slipping fixture.</div>`},
{t:8,w:'Gustavo',q:"How do you structure a CAE report for a design review?",a:`<ol><li><strong>Question and answer in one line:</strong> &ldquo;Passes sag target with 15% margin; fails overslam at hinge reinforcement by 10%.&rdquo;</li><li>Load cases, targets and status table (green, amber, red).</li><li>Key plots: deformation first, then stress or strain at the governing location.</li><li>Assumptions: <em>measured vs assumed</em>, model limitations.</li><li>Recommendation with options and their mass or cost impact.</li><li>Appendix: model checks, mesh study, energy plots.</li></ol>`},
{t:8,w:'Any',q:"What sensors and measurements would you want on a physical test of a panel?",a:`<p>Load cell on the actuator, displacement (LVDT, laser) at the loading point and at the target point, strain gauges (rosettes) at predicted hot spots but not in steep gradients, accelerometers for modal or slam, high-speed video for impact, and DIC for full-field strain on composite or plastic parts. Measured thickness and mass of the actual specimen. Place them using a pre-test simulation.</p>`},
{t:8,w:'Ivan',q:"What is MAC, and what values do you expect?",a:`<p>Modal Assurance Criterion: the normalised squared dot product between a test mode-shape vector and an FE mode-shape vector at the measured points. 1 means identical shape, 0 means orthogonal. Typically &gt; about 0.9 is a good match for the main modes, with off-diagonal values low. Combine with a frequency error target of a few percent.</p>`},

/* ---------- 9 · HARD / PERSONAL ---------- */
{t:9,w:'Gustavo',q:"You joined Helsing a month ago. Why are you talking to us?",a:`<p>Full page: <em>! The Helsing question</em>. 35 seconds, true, consistent with what you told Ana. The process started before Helsing. Helsing is design and you want analysis. No criticism.</p>`},
{t:9,w:'Gustavo',q:"Six months at Tesla, a month at Helsing. Why should we believe you'd stay?",a:`<div class="say">Fair question. The pattern I'd point to is that both times the mismatch was about how much of the job was the work I'm best at. This time I'm moving towards that work, not away from a company. And a car programme runs long. You learn the most by staying to see the next one, and that's what I'm planning on.</div><p>Then stop. Don't add a third justification.</p>`},
{t:9,w:'Gustavo',q:"This salary is a lot lower than Munich. Have you considered that?",a:`<div class="say">Yes, I've seen the posted range. The reason I'm here is the work, and I'd rather go through the package properly with HR.</div><p class="catch">Only if true. See <em>&euro; Money &amp; the move</em>.</p>`},
{t:9,w:'Ivan',q:"Tell us about a time your model was wrong.",a:`<p>Use <em>&sect;5 Story 5</em>: the assumed boundary condition on the Tesla end effector, a week to isolate it, and since then you state measured vs assumed explicitly and treat BCs as the first suspect. Ninety seconds.</p>`},
{t:9,w:'Ivan',q:"What would you not trust in your own thesis model?",a:`<p>This is a test of honesty. Name a real limit: validity outside the tested strain-rate range, a loading mode not tested (e.g. off-axis or combined loading), failure prediction versus pre-failure stiffness, temperature or state-of-charge effects, or calibration without a held-out validation test. Then say what you would do: an extra test, or a range statement in the report.</p>
<div class="say">I'd trust it in the loading regime and rate range I tested, and I'd be careful extrapolating beyond that, particularly into [the regime you didn't test]. If I were taking it further, I'd hold out a test case to validate on and extend the rate range.</div>`},
{t:9,w:'Gustavo',q:"Your manager wants the result tomorrow; you need three days to be confident. What do you do?",a:`<div class="say">I'd give them something tomorrow, but labelled for what it is: a coarse answer with its confidence stated, which parts are checked and which aren't, and what could change it. Often a coarse result already answers the decision. If the answer sits near the limit, I'd say clearly that I need the extra days and why. The thing I won't do is present an unchecked result as if it were final.</div>`},
{t:9,w:'Any',q:"What is the weakest part of your profile for this role?",a:`<div class="say">Volume of industrial CAE. My analysis depth is real, but it comes from one industry thesis, academic projects and analysis done inside design jobs, not from years of running dozens of load cases on a vehicle programme. And I haven't used HyperWorks in production. Both are things this role fixes quickly, and the fundamentals underneath them are the part that's hard to teach.</div>`},
{t:9,w:'Gustavo',q:"When could you start?",a:`<p>Give the real number. Check your Helsing contract tonight (probation notice is commonly two weeks under &sect;622(3) BGB, but the contract decides), then add relocation time. Do not commit to a date you haven't checked.</p>`}
];

/* ---------------- drills ---------------- */
const DRILLS=[
{h:"1 · Explicit stable time step: steel, aluminium, CFRP, 5 mm shell",b:`<div class="say">The time step is the element length divided by the wave speed. For a 5 mm steel or aluminium shell that's just under a microsecond, so a 100 ms slam event is about a hundred thousand steps.</div>
<div class="eq">shell wave speed  c = sqrt( E / (rho (1 - nu^2)) )
steel  E=210 GPa rho=7850  nu=0.30  -> c = 5,420 m/s
alu    E= 70 GPa rho=2700  nu=0.33  -> c = 5,390 m/s
CFRP   quasi-iso E=50 GPa rho=1550  -> c = 5,950 m/s
       UD fibre dir E1=135 GPa      -> c ~ 9,300 m/s (bar)

dt = Le / c   (Le = 5 mm)
steel  0.005/5420 = 0.92 us  x 0.9 safety -> 0.83 us
alu    0.93 us                           -> 0.83 us
CFRP   0.84 us                           -> 0.76 us</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catches:</strong> (1) steel and aluminium have nearly the same wave speed: E/&rho; is almost equal, which surprises people. (2) Stiff fibre directions shrink the time step. (3) Nearly incompressible rubber or seals: the wave speed is driven by the <em>bulk</em> modulus, which is very large, so they can control the time step despite a low shear modulus. (4) The smallest element controls everything.</p>`},
{h:"2 · Mass scaling: what does it cost to reach a 1 µs target?",b:`<div class="say">The time step scales with the square root of density. To make a 2 mm steel element run at 1 microsecond you'd have to scale its density by about eight. That's fine for a handful of tiny elements, but if those elements matter dynamically, you've changed the physics.</div>
<div class="eq">dt ∝ Le * sqrt(rho / E)
2 mm steel: dt = 0.002/5420 x 0.9 = 0.33 us
to reach 1.0 us:  scale = (1.0/0.33)^2 ≈ 9  -> rho x 9

check: added mass / total mass  (keep small, commonly < 2-5 %)
       added mass location (not in the impact path)</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> mass scaling in the impact zone changes peak force and timing. Always report added mass, and for quasi-static runs, also check kinetic energy.</p>`},
{h:"3 · Rear wing: downforce and airbrake load (assumed numbers)",b:`<p>These are illustration values, not Tourbillon data.</p>
<div class="eq">q = 0.5 * rho * v^2
250 km/h (69.4 m/s): q = 0.5*1.225*69.4^2 = 2,950 Pa
300 km/h (83.3 m/s): q = 4,250 Pa
400 km/h (111 m/s):  q = 7,560 Pa

downforce   L = q * CL * S      CL=1.0, S=0.5 m^2 @250 -> ~1.5 kN
airbrake    D = q * CD * A      CD=1.2, A=0.35 m^2 @300 -> ~1.8 kN
step load on lightly damped SDOF: dynamic amplification -> up to ~2
-> design check ~3.5 kN on mounts + actuator, plus fatigue per deployment</div>
<div class="say">Order of magnitude: one or two kilonewtons on a wing that weighs a few kilograms, applied suddenly in the airbrake case. So the dynamic amplification factor matters, and stiffness of the mounts matters, both for strength and so the aero shape holds.</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> force scales with v&sup2;, so 10% more speed is 21% more load. Mention yaw and crosswind asymmetry.</p>`},
{h:"4 · First mode of a CFRP panel (simply supported plate)",b:`<div class="eq">f11 = (pi/2) * (1/a^2 + 1/b^2) * sqrt( D / (rho*h) )
D = E h^3 / (12 (1 - nu^2))

a=0.6 m, b=0.4 m, h=2 mm, E=50 GPa, nu=0.3, rho=1550
D = 50e9 * 8e-9 / (12*0.91) = 36.6 N·m
rho*h = 3.1 kg/m^2   ->  sqrt(D/rho h) = 3.44
1/a^2 + 1/b^2 = 2.78 + 6.25 = 9.03
f11 = 1.571 * 9.03 * 3.44 ≈ 49 Hz</div>
<div class="say">Around 50 Hz for a 600 by 400 by 2 mm quasi-isotropic carbon panel, simply supported. Clamped edges roughly double it. Halve the thickness and the frequency halves, because D goes with h cubed and mass with h, so f is proportional to h.</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> f &prop; h (not h&sup3;) for plates. Curvature raises it a lot, which is why flat panels are the problem panels.</p>`},
{h:"5 · Mirror arm: tip stiffness and first mode",b:`<div class="eq">aluminium arm L=150 mm, section 20 x 10 mm (bending about the thin axis)
I = b h^3 / 12 = 0.02 * 0.01^3 / 12 = 1.67e-9 m^4
k = 3 E I / L^3 = 3*70e9*1.67e-9 / 0.15^3 = 104 kN/m
tip load 50 N -> delta = 0.48 mm
head mass m = 1 kg (arm mass neglected)
f = (1/2pi) * sqrt(k/m) = (1/6.28)*sqrt(104,000) ≈ 51 Hz</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catches:</strong> L&sup3;: a 20% longer arm loses about 42% stiffness. The mounting compliance (door or body) often dominates the real result, so model the base, not a fixed constraint.</p>`},
{h:"6 · Door sag: hinge reactions (assumed numbers)",b:`<div class="eq">door m = 18 kg, CG 0.6 m from hinge line
sag load P = 900 N at 1.1 m
M = 18*9.81*0.6 + 900*1.1 = 106 + 990 = 1,096 N·m
hinge spacing s = 0.35 m -> axial couple F = M/s = 3.1 kN per hinge
(+ vertical shear shared: (18*9.81 + 900)/2 ≈ 540 N each)</div>
<div class="say">The sag load, not the door weight, dominates the hinge moment. Close hinge spacing turns it into large hinge forces, which is why the hinge reinforcement and the pillar stiffness matter as much as the hinge itself.</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Dihedral:</strong> the hinge axis is inclined and the moment arm of gravity changes with opening angle. Check several angles.</p>`},
{h:"7 · Bolt preload and composite fixings",b:`<div class="eq">T = K * F * d        K ≈ 0.2 (dry steel, nut factor)
M8 at 25 N·m:  F = 25 / (0.2*0.008) ≈ 15.6 kN
under head area (M8, ~13 mm OD washer, 8.4 mm hole) ≈ 77 mm^2
bearing pressure ≈ 15,600 / 77 ≈ 200 MPa  -> too high for bare CFRP
-> metallic insert / compression limiter / larger washer</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> K varies from about 0.1 to 0.3 with lubrication, so the preload scatter is &plusmn;25% or more. Composites creep and relax under preload, so clamp load is lost over time. Modelling: beam plus pretension (e.g. PRETENS in OptiStruct) with contact on the clamped faces.</p>`},
{h:"8 · Fatigue: how sensitive is life to stress?",b:`<div class="eq">Basquin form:  N ∝ S^(-k)
k ≈ 3 for welded joints, ≈ 5-10 for smooth parts
10 % stress increase, k=5:  life x (1/1.1)^5 = 0.62  (-38 %)
double stress,     k=5:  life / 32

Miner:  D = sum(n_i / N_i)  ; failure at D ≈ 1 (often target D < 0.3-0.5)</div>
<div class="say">Fatigue life is very sensitive to stress: ten percent more stress costs about a third of the life for a smooth part. That's why mesh convergence at the hotspot matters more for durability than for static strength.</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> mean-stress correction (Goodman is conservative, Gerber less so) and surface, size and notch factors. Composites and adhesives use different fatigue approaches from metals.</p>`},
{h:"9 · Thermal mismatch: aluminium bracket on a CFRP panel",b:`<div class="eq">delta L = alpha * L * delta T,   L = 1.0 m, delta T = 80 K
aluminium  23e-6  -> 1.84 mm
CFRP (q-i) ~1-2e-6 -> 0.08-0.16 mm
mismatch ≈ 1.7 mm  (visible as gap / flush change)

fully constrained aluminium: sigma = E alpha dT = 70e3*23e-6*80 ≈ 129 MPa</div>
<div class="say">Almost two millimetres of mismatch over a metre. That is visible on a car where gaps are measured in tenths, and if you constrain it rigidly, the bracket sees well over a hundred MPa just from temperature. So you design the fixing to float, or match the CTE.</div>`},
{h:"10 · Low-speed impact energy and mean force",b:`<div class="eq">E = 0.5 m v^2     m = 2,000 kg (assumed), v = 4 km/h = 1.11 m/s
E = 0.5 * 2000 * 1.235 ≈ 1.2 kJ
absorbed over 50 mm stroke at constant force: F = E/d ≈ 25 kN  (~1.3 g)
over 25 mm: 50 kN -> halving the stroke doubles the force</div>
<p class="catch"><strong>Catch:</strong> on a hypercar with a low front and stiff carbon, the stroke available before hitting a hard point is small. That drives the absorber design. Use this as a sanity check on explicit results: does the energy absorbed in the model match &frac12;mv&sup2;?</p>`},
{h:"11 · Read this energy report: is the run acceptable?",b:`<div class="eq">initial KE          12.0 kJ
final internal      10.2 kJ
final KE             0.5 kJ
hourglass            1.4 kJ   <-
contact (sliding)   -0.2 kJ   <-
added mass           3 %  of total
total energy drift  +0.1 kJ</div>
<div class="say">Total energy is conserved, so the run is stable. But hourglass energy is about fourteen percent of internal, which is too high. The deformation is partly non-physical. And negative contact energy suggests penetrations or a bad initial contact. Three percent added mass is acceptable if it's not in the crush zone. I'd look at where the hourglass energy is concentrated, refine or change the hourglass control there, check initial penetrations, and rerun before I'd report anything.</div>`},
{h:"12 · Oil-canning: why Newton–Raphson fails, and how to fix it",b:`<div class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 420 180" role="img" aria-label="snap-through load-deflection curve">
<line x1="40" y1="150" x2="400" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".5"/><line x1="40" y1="150" x2="40" y2="15" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".5"/>
<path d="M40,150 C90,90 120,45 160,45 C200,45 220,110 260,115 C300,118 330,70 395,25" fill="none" stroke="#5b9dff" stroke-width="2.5"/>
<line x1="160" y1="45" x2="355" y2="45" stroke="#ff6b6b" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>
<text x="165" y="38" fill="currentColor" font-size="11">limit point</text>
<text x="270" y="60" fill="#ff6b6b" font-size="11">load control jumps</text>
<text x="300" y="168" fill="currentColor" font-size="11">indenter displacement</text>
<text x="46" y="26" fill="currentColor" font-size="11">force</text>
</svg></div>
<div class="say">A shallow panel under an indenter stiffens, reaches a limit point, then the force drops while displacement increases. That's the snap-through, the oil-can. With load control, Newton&ndash;Raphson can't go past the peak: the tangent stiffness goes to zero and the solution either diverges or jumps to a different branch. Displacement control on the indenter traces the drop. If there's snap-back, where displacement also reverses, you need arc-length. The criterion is usually that no negative slope or flat region appears up to the specified load.</div>`}
];
